Abstract
Brain tumour classification from Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI) images is an important application of deep learning in medical image analysis. This project compares four architectures—Multilayer Perceptron (MLP), ResNet18, CNN-LSTM, and Vision Transformer (ViT)—for multi-class brain tumour classification. At this report revision, full-split test evaluations are available for all four models on the same held-out split. The MLP, CNN-LSTM, and compact ViT runs are short preliminary CPU experiments, not optimized benchmarks.
The study uses the publicly available MRI Brain Tumour Image Dataset compiled by Nickparvar. The audited copy used in the repository contained 7,200 files; after excluding 203 filenames containing "aug", 6,997 images remain in the four classes: Glioma, Meningioma, Pituitary Tumour, and No Tumour.
A common preprocessing and experimental framework is used to facilitate a controlled comparison. The images are resized and normalized before model training and evaluation. The selected architectures represent different approaches to visual representation learning: the MLP provides a fully connected baseline, ResNet18 uses convolutional residual learning, CNN-LSTM combines spatial feature extraction with sequential modelling, and ViT uses patch-based representations and self-attention.
Model performance is compared using accuracy, precision, recall, F1-score, class-wise confusion patterns, parameter count, and computational requirements. The study analyses how architectural differences influence classification performance under a common experimental setup; the short training schedules, particularly for ViT, limit conclusions about fully optimized performance.

Introduction

Brain tumours are abnormal growths of cells within or around the brain and can vary substantially in their location, morphology, progression, and clinical significance. Accurate identification of tumour type is important for subsequent clinical assessment and treatment planning. Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI) is widely used for examining intracranial abnormalities because it provides detailed soft-tissue contrast and allows structural differences within the brain to be visualized without the use of ionizing radiation. However, manual examination of MRI scans requires specialized expertise and can be time-consuming. Deep learning-based image classification can therefore serve as a computer-aided approach for automatically identifying patterns associated with different brain tumour categories.
The present project investigates multi-class classification of brain MRI images into four categories: Glioma, Meningioma, Pituitary Tumour, and No Tumour. The project uses the publicly available Brain Tumour MRI Dataset by Nickparvar, which combines images from multiple public sources, including Figshare, SARTAJ, and Br35H [1]. The audited repository copy contained 7,200 files. Excluding 203 files whose names contain "aug" leaves 6,997 images for the fixed experiment split; this explains the difference from the dataset's commonly reported 7,023-image count.
Although deep learning models have demonstrated strong performance in medical image classification, different architectures process image information in fundamentally different ways. Consequently, evaluating models under different preprocessing procedures, dataset partitions, or evaluation protocols can make direct comparison difficult. This project addresses this issue by comparing four architectures representing distinct deep learning families under a common experimental setup: a Multilayer Perceptron (MLP), ResNet18, a CNN-LSTM hybrid, and a Vision Transformer (ViT).
The MLP serves as the classical baseline and processes a flattened representation of the MRI image without explicitly modelling two-dimensional spatial relationships. ResNet18 represents convolutional neural networks and uses convolutional filters together with residual connections to learn hierarchical spatial features [4]. The CNN-LSTM architecture combines convolutional feature extraction with Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) units, allowing spatial image features to be represented as a sequence and subsequently modelled through recurrent processing [5]. Finally, the Vision Transformer divides an image into patches and applies self-attention to model relationships between different image regions [7].
To ensure a meaningful comparison, all four models are evaluated using a consistent dataset partition, preprocessing pipeline, and evaluation protocol. Performance is assessed using classification accuracy, precision, recall, F1-score, class-wise errors, and computational characteristics. This enables the study to investigate not only which architectures provide strong predictive performance but also how their different architectural assumptions influence their behaviour on brain MRI images.
The scope of the project is restricted to image-level multi-class brain tumour classification. It does not attempt tumour segmentation, tumour grading, survival prediction, or clinical diagnosis. Instead, the project provides a controlled experimental comparison of representative classical, convolutional, recurrent, and attention-based deep learning architectures. The resulting analysis is intended to provide insight into the strengths, limitations, and computational trade-offs of different deep learning approaches for automated brain MRI classification.

Dataset
